      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2016 Elsevier Ireland Ltd. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "INTRODUCTION",
            "content": "We examine serum levels sTNFR-I and sTNFR-II in endometriosis patients, and their role as biomarkers of endometriosis."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "MATERIAL AND METHODS",
            "content": "Women were diagnosed with endometriosis during laparoscopy to investigate pelvic pain and/or infertility (N=62). Control group included women with pelvic pain and/or infertility, whose laparoscopy showed no abnormalities (N=55). Serum concentrations of sTNFR-I and sTNFR-II were measured using Bioplex Protein Array system. Non-parametric statistics were used."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Endometriosis patients had significantly higher levels of sTNFR-I than controls (257.46pg/ml, IQR=2.37-1048.92 versus 130.39pg/ml, IQR=0.99-361.1 respectively, P value=0.01). For TNFR-II, difference between women with (232pg/ml, IQR=0.0-624.4), and women without (132.93pg/ml, IQR=0.0-312.81) endometriosis was not significant (P value=0.05). Early stage endometriosis patients had significantly higher level of sTNFR-I (559.13, IQR=1.82-1289.86) and sTNFR-II (248.8, IQR=0-644.65) than control women (P value is 0.01 for TNFR-I and 0.04 for TNFR-II). Levels of sTNFR-I and sTNFR-II were comparable for advanced endometriosis and controls, and between early and advanced endometriosis. As a biomarker for all- stage endometriosis, sTNFR-I produces AUC of 0.62, sensitivity of 61%, and specificity of 47.3%, at a cutoff of 81.87pg/ml. For early stage disease, sTNFR-I yields AUC of 0.68, sensitivity of 60.7%, specificity of 75%, at a cutoff of 351.22pg/ml."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "sTNFR-I is significantly higher in serum of endometriosis patients than controls. As an endometriosis biomarker, sTNFR-I achieves better performance for early stage disease."
          }
        ]
      },
